-When two servers communicate, they use asymmetric encryption to securely verify identities and establish trust, then immediately switch to symmetric encryption to rapidly transfer the bulk data.

a. Server A sends clientHello along with its supported TLS versions, a list of cypher suites and a temp "session key" share.
b. Server B confirms TLS and cipher suite to use,  a temp "session key" share, and sends its public key cert to server A everything (signed) with its private key.
c. Server A validates the signed message with the public key.
d. Symmectric session is established combining both temp session keys (symmetric key). now the secure tunnel is established and all data
is encrypted using the shared symmetric key.
 
1. Server A creates a temporary, random "session key" and encrypts it using Server B's public key. Only Server B can decrypt this secret session key using its private key.

2. All subsequent API calls, database transfers, or microservice payloads are encrypted and decrypted using the lightning-fast symmetric key.

When server A tries to talk to server B and both having the same Root internal CA, server B needs to have intermediate and the leaf certificate.. 
server A will receive the leaf certificate + intermediate from server B to establish secure communication chain validation path

*** an intermediate certificate bridges the gap between a leaf certificate and the root CA.. so the leaf certificate is signed by the intermediate certificate,
and the intermediate certificate is signed by the root CA.

*** Server A checks its own local tamper-proof store, finds "Root Y", and says: "I trust Root Y, therefore I trust Intermediate X, which means I can safely trust this Leaf Certificate."

CRL (Certificate Revocation List): A large, downloadable file containing all revoked certificates from a specific CA.
